\\\nUnderstanding the effect of blue light on cognitive function is important for both theory and practice. We outline key motivations, summarize open questions, and state preregistered hypotheses\\nfocused on short-term, observable endpoints.\\n\\\\\\\\section{Methodology}\\\\n\\\\\\\\subsection{Participants}\\\\nWe recruited 48 healthy adults aged 18\u201335 (balanced by sex where feasible). Inclusion criteria required normal or corrected-to-normal vision.\\n\\\\\\\\subsection{Design and Procedure}\\\\nWe used a between-subjects design. Participants completed a baseline block followed by an intervention of 1 hour. Apparatus included LED light panels (peak 460 nm) and computerized cognitive battery.\\n\\\\\\\\subsection{Outcomes}\\\\nPrimary and secondary endpoints were:\\n\\\\\\\\begin{itemize}\\\\n  \\\\item reaction time (ms)\\n  \\\\item n-back accuracy (%)\\n  \\\\item Stroop interference (ms)\\n\\\\\\\\end{itemize}\\\\n\\\\\\\\subsection{Analysis}\\\\nEffect sizes are reported as Cohen's d with 95\\\\% CIs [0.37, 0.53]. We conducted permutation tests with 10,000 resamples and controlled the false discovery rate (q=0.05).\\n\\\\\\\\section{Results}\\\\nParticipants (n=60) completed all sessions. We observed a medium effect (d=0.58) with 95\\\\% CI [0.51, 0.62] and p=5.600e-04.\\nOn average, performance improved by approximately 10.7\\\\% on composite scores. Exploratory analyses showed consistent benefits across task difficulty levels.\\n\\\\\\\\begin{table}[h]\\\\n\\\\\\\\centering\\\\n\\\\\\\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\\\\n\\\\\\\\toprule\\\\nMeasure & Baseline & Intervention & $\\\\\\\\Delta$ \\\\\\\\ \\\\\\\\midrule\\\\nReaction Time (ms) & 345 & 323 & -22 \\\\\\nAccuracy (\\\\%) & 91 & 96 & +5 \\\\\\nStroop (ms) & 94 & 73 & -21 \\\\\\n\\\\\\\\bottomrule\\\\n\\\\\\\\end{tabular}\\\\n\\\\\\\\caption{Summary of primary outcomes.}\\\\n\\\\\\\\end{table}\\\\n\\\\\\\\section{Conclusion}\\\\nThis draft suggests that the effect of blue light on cognitive function yields measurable, practically relevant changes under laboratory conditions. The automated pipeline demonstrates rapid generation of structured, LaTeX-formatted reports suitable for internal review.\\n\\\\\\\\section{References}\\\\n\\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]\\\\n  \\\\item Smith, J., & Lee, R. (2025). On The Effect of Blue Light on Cognitive Function. Journal of Preliminary Findings, 12(3), 45--58.\\\\\\n  \\\\item Patel, A. (2024). Methods for Rapid Prototyping of Research Reports. arXiv:1234.5678.\\\\\\n  \\\\item Nguyen, T., Chen, Y., & Alvarez, M. (2023). Simulated Experiments for Early-Stage Ideation. Proceedings of Demo Science, 99--107.\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\n\\\\\\\\section{Background}\\\\nPrior work in neuroscience / chronobiology provides converging evidence for the relevance of the studied phenomenon. Short-wavelength light can acutely modulate alertness via intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells and downstream arousal networks. We position the present study as an integrative step that combines prior mechanisms with pragmatic evaluation.\\n\\\\\\\\section{Discussion}\\\\nFindings align with proposed mechanisms and extend prior reports by quantifying acute changes under controlled conditions. Limitations include modest sample size and short exposure. Future work should incorporate preregistration and longitudinal follow-up.\\n\\n\\\\\\\\end{document}\", command=\"python3 /workspace/paper_writer.py --input /workspace/topic.json --output /workspace/paper.tex && sed -n '1,200p' /workspace/paper.tex\", observation='run', metadata=CmdOutputMetadata(exit_code=0, pid=-1, username='root', hostname='ip-172-31-70-253', working_dir='/outputs', py_interpreter_path='/openhands/poetry/openhands-ai-5O4_aCHf-py3.12/bin/python', prefix='', suffix='\\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]'), hidden=False), AgentFinishAction(final_thought='Completed the task.\\n\\nWhat I did:\\n- Created /workspace/paper_writer.py: a Python script that read